the last of us captured plenty of parts  when it launched in 2013 but even if  you're playing it for the first time in  2020 like I am Jolla Nellie's  cross-country journey is just as moving  and impactful here's why while there are  always elements of games that are bound  to date after a long enough period of  time The Last of Us is greatest strength  is timeless that is its betrayal of  complex characters embroiled in an  emotional and heart-wrenching fight for  survival it's the things that don't age  the game's performances pacing and  excellent use of the linear narrative  format that keep the magic of Naughty  Dog's post-apocalyptic epic intact seven  years later I played an hour or two of  The Last of Us when it first came out  but somewhere in the brick littered  arenas and conveniently placed chest  high walls it lost me that isn't to say  the beginning didn't absolutely break my  heart though it remains one of the best  interest to a game I've ever seen  but it took me until this year to give  the game a second chance and really get  into it The Last of Us isn't framed  around boss encounters or becoming an  unstoppable powerhouse the experience  thrives on instilling a strong sense of  vulnerability you spend the entire game  scrambling pushing through one tense and  uncertain situation into the next and  the feeling that that brings you as a  player is very similar to the state Joel  and Ellie find themselves in throughout  the game the mechanics of the Last of Us  elevate its themes of survival and  desperation keeping resources sparse  inventory limits low and crafting items  essential enforces the idea that you're  fighting tooth and nail to survive  against portal odds this sort of shared  experience between the player and  characters creates empathy that enhances  the impact of the emotional story The  Last of Us presses its troubled  characters into horrible situations  forces them to persevere through tragedy  and ultimately that changes who they are  and the relationships they form the  game's biggest success is in how it  India's you to its characters brutal as  they can be encouraging you to care for  them and want to know where their story  leads the Last of Us leverages a linear  narrative and a very deliberate pace to  drive its most impactful scenes and  character moments the game school  underpins and elevates each moment as it  runs the gamut from severe  attention to heart-pounding gunfights -  heartfelt dialogues using the linear  narrative format not only lets Naughty  Dog tell the story it wants to but have  you experienced it in the precise way  the developer wants you to in my opinion  there's no better way to tell an  affecting videogames story especially if  it's a story worth telling there are so  many touches that make the last of us  feel especially alive and impactful  collectable notes left behind encourage  you to delve deeper into the game world  and inform what's happened to the people  you encounter and how the world has been  reshaped by the outbreak Ellie's  tendency to independently knows her way  around the game world makes it feel like  you're with an ally and not escorting an  NPC most obviously the game's  performances remain as excellent today  as they were seven years ago and elevate  the characters above and beyond the  writing The Last of Us isn't a game to  be won there's room for multiple  approaches in combat and whether you're  a gun-toting hero who shoots their way  through every arena or more like me  taking five times as long to crawl  stealthily through there's no right or  wrong way to go about it the game isn't  interested in rewarding or punishing you  for your preferred approach to combat it  just wants you to keep moving ahead  whether you finish a fight stocked up on  health or managed by the skin of your  teeth just like Joel and Ellie survival  is what matters not how you survive The  Last of Us makes your actions a  narrative device making you part of the  story in its final moments it comes full  circle in making you feel what it's  characters feel going beyond just a  well-made zombie apocalypse story the  further you get through the game world  the more you understand the depth of its  brutality and how it has changed the  characters trying to survive within it  but it also shines a light on how and  why characters hold tight to the things  that matter to them and as a result it's  shocking final moments feel earned truly  brilliant stories are timeless  especially those presented in compelling  ways it's why the Last of Us became so  iconic and why there's such excitement  for the sequel  is the case for Joel and Ellie it  doesn't matter how you make it to the  end it just matters that you do and  theirs is a story that deserves to be  seen through  you
 

